<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanesh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kültepe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kanesh, modern Kültepe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, was an ancient city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. In the early second millennium BCE it hosted the most important Assyrian merchant colony, or karum. Thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets found there document the trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and textiles for Anatolian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> that underpinned early Assyrian power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanesh.jpg" alt="Kanesh" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Klaus-Peter Simon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%BCltepeOberstadtPalast.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kültepe, Turkish for ash-hill, lies about 20 km northeast of the modern city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a> on the Central Anatolian plateau. The site has two parts: a circular mound, about 500 m across and some 20 m high, and a lower town to its northeast. The mound was inhabited, with breaks, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, while the lower town was occupied only from the last decades of the third millennium BCE to the early sixteenth century BCE. Its ancient name appears as Kaneš in Assyrian inscriptions of the 20th and 19th centuries BCE and as Neša in later Hittite ones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early second millennium BCE a kārum, the Assyrian word for a trading district, was established in the lower town. Local officials set it aside for Assyrian merchants, who could trade there without paying taxes as long as the goods stayed inside. Soldiers and merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> lived there for hundreds of years, exchanging local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and wool for luxury goods, foodstuffs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and woven fabrics from the Assyrian homeland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>.</p><p>It appears to have been the administrative and distribution centre of the whole network of Assyrian colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and the trade was run by families rather than the state. The kārum was destroyed by fire at the end of its two main levels, II and Ib, and its inhabitants left most of their possessions behind.</p><p>Local kings reigned at Kaneš in this period, among them Ḫurmili, Paḫanu, Inar and Waršama. Uḫna, king of Zalpuwa, raided the city and carried off its Šiuš idol. Pitḫana, king of Kuššara, then conquered Neša in the night, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anitta">Anitta</a> made it his capital and recovered the idol. In the 17th century BCE Anitta's descendants moved their capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>, founding the line of Hittite kings, and the Hittite language was called Nešili, the Neša tongue.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Over 22,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets have been recovered, mainly from the kārum, and only about forty from the mound. Written in Old Assyrian, they record trade between the colony and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> and between merchants and local people, and they are the oldest documents from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Their Hittite loanwords and names are the oldest record of any Indo-European language. The letters of women such as the businesswomen Zizizi and Taram-Kubi show their part in the trade. Ernest Chantre began excavating in 1893, Bedřich Hrozný found over 1,000 tablets in 1925, and Tahsin Özgüç led the Turkish excavations begun in 1948 until 2005. The site was placed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s tentative World Heritage list in 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kültepe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It rested on a network of Assyrian merchant colonies in Anatolia, of which the karum at <b>Kanesh</b> (modern Kültepe in Turkey) was the most important.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Assyrian merchants of the early 2nd millennium BCE carried it to Anatolia, where some 23,000 of their letters and contracts have been found at <b>Kanesh</b>, near present-day Kayseri in Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 2nd millennium BCE it was one of the native Anatolian kingdoms competing for power with Kanesh, and the Hittites remembered it in a legend of thirty sons of the queen of <b>Kanesh</b> who drifted there in baskets.</blockquote><a href="/state/zalpa">Zalpa</a></li></ul></section>
